Exercise: Limitations and Verification - mergeSort Bug*

*Bug:* Line `j++` should be `i++` in leftover left loop - causes infinite loop.

*AI Fix:* Change to `i++`

*1. Collaborative Verification:*
- Understanding: i never increments -> infinite loop
- Tests: `[3][1][2]` => `[1][2][3]`, `[5][4][3][2][1]` => `[1][2][3][4][5]`, `[]` => `[]`, `[2][2][1]` => `[1][2][2]`
- Verification: Ran tests with timeout - passes after fix, hangs before

*2. Alternative Approaches:*
- *Fix 1 (minimal):* `i++` - fastest
- *Fix 2 (safe):* `return result.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j))` - no counter bug possible, more readable
- Best: Fix 2 for production, Fix 1 for interview

*3. Critical Eye:*
- Weakness: Infinite loop crashes browser, not just wrong result
- Assumption: Input is numbers, uses `<` not `<=` so unstable
- Improvement: Use `<=` for stable sort + concat to prevent future j++/i++ bug

*Final Verified Code:*
function merge(left, right) {
  let result = [], i=0, j=0;
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] <= right[j]) result.push(left[i++]);
    else result.push(right[j++]);
  }
  while (i < left.length) { result.push(left[i]); i++; } // FIXED: was j++
  while (j < right.length) { result.push(right[j]); j++; }
  return result;
}
